#answer("lp-sheet-1")[*Variables:* $x_(p w) >= 0$ = units of product $p$ made at workstation $w$ ($p = 1..3$, $w = 1..4$); the same product may be split over workstations. *Objective:* $max sum_(p, w) "profit"_(p w) x_(p w)$. *Capacity* (minutes): $sum_p "time"_(p w) x_(p w) <= 35 dot 60 = 2100$ for each $w$. *Demand:* $sum_w x_(p w) >= 100, 150, 100$ for $p = 1, 2, 3$. CPLEX: profit 15 740.83 €; product 1 only at WS3 (100), product 3 only at WS4 (100), product 2 everywhere else (350, 175, 212.5, 26.67 units on WS1–WS4). Note that the demands are minima, not exact amounts: product 2 is produced far beyond 150 because it is the most profitable per minute.]

#answer("lp-sheet-3")[*Variables:* $x_(s p) >= 0$ = tons from grower $s in {1, 2, 3}$ to plant $p in {A, B}$. *Objective:* each ton earns $50 - "price"_s - "ship"_(s p) - "labour"_p$:
$ max sum_(s, p) (50 - pi_s - sigma_(s p) - lambda_p) x_(s p). $
*Supply:* $sum_p x_(s p) <= 200, 310, 420$. *Capacity:* $sum_s x_(s A) <= 460$, $sum_s x_(s B) <= 560$. (One ton of fruit gives one ton of canned fruit, so no balance variable is needed.) Optimum 13 070 €: S1→A 200, S2→A 170, S2→B 140, S3→B 420 — all supply used, plant B not full.]

#answer("lp-sheet-6")[Variables $x_A, dots, x_E >= 0$ in M€. (a) $max 0.043 x_A + 0.027 x_B + 0.025 x_C + 0.022 x_D + 0.045 x_E$ s.t. $sum x <= 10$, $x_C + x_D >= 4$, $2 x_A + 5 x_B + x_C + x_D + 3 x_E <= 1.4 sum x$ (ratio cleared). Optimum 0.322 M€ = 322 k€ with $x_A = 4$, $x_C = 6$ (average risk exactly 1.4). (b) Add $w in [0, 1]$ borrowed: $sum x <= 10 + w$ and subtract $0.0275 w$ in the objective. Optimum 326.7 k€: borrow the full 1 M€, $x_A = 4.4$, $x_C = 6.6$ (the bond return beats 2.75 % on average). (c) Minimizing a ratio is not linear in general. The natural reading is that the whole 10 M€ is invested, $sum x = 10$; then the average risk is $(2 x_A + 5 x_B + x_C + x_D + 3 x_E) slash 10$, linear. Constraints: $x_C + x_D >= 4$, earnings $>= 0.03$. Optimum: average risk 1 — everything in government bonds (for instance 10 M€ in C earns 250 k€, far above 30 k€). If the amount invested were also free, the Charnes–Cooper change of variables ($t = 1 slash sum x$, $u = t x$) would make it linear again; the answer does not change here.]

#answer("lp-sheet-7")[Work in thousands. *Variables:* $s, p, k >= 0$ = thousands of sporks, packets and school packs _produced_. Packets consume sporks and school packs consume both, so the quantities _sold_ are $s - p - 10 k$ loose sporks (each thousand school packs uses 10 thousand sporks) and $p - 100 k$ packets; both must be $>= 0$ (balances). *Objective:* $max 5 (s - p - 10k) + 15 (p - 100 k) + 300 k - 2.5 s - 4 p - 8 k$. *Hours:* the statement is ambiguous. Reading 1 — each resource has 200 h: moulding $0.8 s <= 200$, packaging $1.5 p + 2.5 k <= 200$, supervision $0.2 s + 0.5 p + 0.5 k <= 200$. Optimum 1425 €: $s = 250$, $p = 133.33$, $k = 0$. Reading 2 — 200 h in total: $0.8 s + 1.5 p + 2.5 k + 0.2 s + 0.5 p + 0.5 k <= 200$. Optimum 566.67 €: $s = p = 66.67$, $k = 0$. In both, school packs do not pay: 300 € per thousand does not cover the 100 thousand packets inside them.]

#answer("lp-sheet-8")[*Variables:* $x_1, dots, x_4 >= 0$ units; $t_(B A), t_(C A) >= 0$ hours moved from stage B (resp. C) to stage A. *Objective:* $max 10 x_1 + 15 x_2 + 22 x_3 + 17 x_4$. *Sales:* $x_1 <= 50$, $x_2 <= 60$, $x_3 <= 85$, $x_4 <= 70$. *Stage hours:* A: $2x_1 + 2x_2 + x_3 + x_4 <= 160 + t_(B A) + t_(C A)$; B: $2 x_1 + 4 x_2 + x_3 + 2 x_4 <= 200 - t_(B A)$; C: $3 x_1 + 6 x_2 + x_3 + 5 x_4 <= 80 - t_(C A)$; transfer limits $t_(B A) <= 0.2 dot 200 = 40$, $t_(C A) <= 0.3 dot 80 = 24$. *Ratio:* $0.9 x_4 <= x_1 <= 1.15 x_4$. Optimum 1760 €: 80 units of P3 and nothing else (stage C is the bottleneck and P3 uses only 1 h of it); no transfer is used. Note that the ratio constraint allows $x_1 = x_4 = 0$.]

#answer("lp-sheet-2")[*Variables:* $x_(i j) >= 0$ = tons of cargo $i$ in compartment $j$. *Objective:* $max sum_(i, j) "profit"_i x_(i j)$. *Availability:* $sum_j x_(i j) <= w_i$. *Weight:* $sum_i x_(i j) <= W_j$. *Volume:* $sum_i v_i x_(i j) <= V_j$. *Balance:* $(sum_i x_(i 1)) slash 10 = (sum_i x_(i 2)) slash 16 = (sum_i x_(i 3)) slash 8$, written as two linear equalities $16 sum_i x_(i 1) = 10 sum_i x_(i 2)$ and $8 sum_i x_(i 2) = 16 sum_i x_(i 3)$. Optimum 12 151.58 €: 15 t of C2, 15.947 t of C3, 3.053 t of C4, none of C1; the plane is full (34 t), so every compartment is at 100 % of its weight limit.]

#answer("lp-sheet-4")[*Variables:* $x_1$ = units of P1 (they use both machines); $y_(p A), y_(p B)$ = units of $p in {2, 3, 4}$ made on A or B. Write $X_p = y_(p A) + y_(p B)$. *Objective:* $max 10 x_1 + 12 X_2 + 17 X_3 + 8 X_4$. *Machine A* (minutes): $10 x_1 + 12 y_(2A) + 13 y_(3A) + 8 y_(4A) <= 0.95 dot 35 dot 60 = 1995$. *Machine B:* $27 x_1 + 19 y_(2B) + 33 y_(3B) + 23 y_(4B) <= 0.93 dot 2100 = 1953$. *Floor:* $0.1 x_1 + 0.15 X_2 + 0.5 X_3 + 0.05 X_4 <= 50$. *Proportion:* $0.95 dot 2 X_3 <= X_2 <= 1.05 dot 2 X_3$. Optimum 3439.70 €: no P1, and $X_2 = 102.79$, $X_3 = 52.81$, $X_4 = 163.56$ (how they split between machines is not unique).]

#answer("lp-sheet-5")[*Variables:* $a, b$ (and $c$) *free*; for each point $k$, $e^+_k, e^-_k >= 0$; for (b), (d) one more variable $z$. (a) $min sum_k (e^+_k + e^-_k)$ s.t. $y_k - a x_k - b = e^+_k - e^-_k$. Optimum 11.4663 with, e.g., $y = 0.6375 x + 0.5813$. (b) $min z$ s.t. $-z <= y_k - a x_k - b <= z$. Optimum 1.725 with $y = 0.625 x - 0.4$. (c) As (a) with $a x_k^2 + b x_k + c$ — still linear in $(a, b, c)$ because $x_k^2$ is a number. Optimum 10.459 with $y = 0.0337 x^2 + 0.2945 x + 0.9824$. (d) As (b) with the quadratic: optimum 1.475 with $y = 0.125 x^2 - 0.625 x + 2.475$. The objective values are unique; the fitted coefficients of (a) and (c) need not be.]

#answer("lp-sheet-9")[*Variables:* $h_1 in [0, 100]$, $h_2 in [0, 110]$ hours; $a in [1050, 1200]$ assembled products. Parts made: $P_1 = 7h_1 + 6h_2$, $P_2 = 6h_1 + 11h_2$, $P_3 = 9h_1 + 5h_2$. *Constraints:* $a <= P_i$ for each part; leftovers $sum_i (P_i - a) <= 200$. *Objective:* $min 25 h_1 + 12.5 h_2$. *The oddity:* this instance is infeasible. Minimizing the leftovers instead shows that at least 333.3 parts would remain (at $h_1 = 100$, $h_2 = 58.3$, $a = 1050$): the two departments make the three parts in proportions that never match. If surplus parts may be scrapped (only stored ones count against the 200), add stored-part variables $s_i >= 0$ with $a + s_i <= P_i$, $sum_i s_i <= 200$; then the optimum costs 2767.86 € with $h_1 = 55.71$, $h_2 = 110$, $a = 1050$.]

#answer("ch1-new-exam")[(a) $x_(i k) >= 0$ = bikes sent from depot $i$ to station $k$ (real is fine at this level; integer if asked). (b) $min sum_(i, k) c_(i k) x_(i k)$. (c) Supply: $sum_k x_(i k) <= s_i$ for each depot (cannot send bikes you do not have). Loading: $sum_k x_(i k) <= L$ for each depot. Demand: $sum_i x_(i k) = d_k$ for each station. Depot 1 share: $sum_k x_(1 k) >= 0.4 sum_(i, k) x_(i k)$ (ratio cleared). (d) Demand becomes $sum_i x_(i k) >= d_k$; since bikes cost money to move, the optimum still sends exactly $d_k$ unless the depot-1 share forces extra bikes.]
